::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (days/year) in predicted dates of first adult emergence for SLI across the CONUS over the entire 44-year period (1981–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant.](figure1.png){#fig-sli-emergence fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (days/year) in predicted dates of first egg hatch for SLI across the CONUS over the entire 44-year period (1981–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant.](figure2.png){#fig-sli-egg-hatch fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::


#### **Recent period: 2001--2025**

During 2001–2025, egg hatch showed earlier trends across much of the western and central United States, while adult emergence was later across portions of the northern interior and earlier in scattered southern areas (@fig-sli-emergence-recent, @fig-sli-egg-hatch-recent). Significant changes were spatially limited.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (days/year) in predicted dates of first adult emergence for SLI across the CONUS over the recent 24-year period (2001–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant.](figure3.png){#fig-sli-emergence-recent fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (days/year) in predicted dates of first egg hatch for SLI across the CONUS over the recent 24-year period (2001–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant.](figure4.png){#fig-sli-egg-hatch-recent fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::


### **ii. Climatic suitability**

#### **Entire period: 1981--2025**

Predicted cold stress decreased across much of the northern United States over 1981–2025, while heat stress changes were highly localized and concentrated mainly in portions of the Southwest (@fig-sli-cold-stress, @fig-sli-heat-stress). Predicted climatic suitability showed very little statistically significant change (@fig-sli-suitability).


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (units/year) in predicted cold stress for SLI across the CONUS over the entire 44-year period (1981–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant. Areas shown as 0 in (B) had significant trends but very small changes.](figure5.png){#fig-sli-cold-stress fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (units/year) in predicted heat stress for SLI across the CONUS over the entire 44-year period (1981–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant. Areas shown as 0 in (B) had significant trends but very small changes.](figure6.png){#fig-sli-heat-stress fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes in predicted climatic suitability for SLI across the CONUS over the 44-year period (1981–2025). Suitability is defined as areas not excluded by climate stress. Positive changes (warmer colors) indicate an increased likelihood of establishment, whereas negative changes (cooler colors) indicate a decreased likelihood of establishment.](figure7.png){#fig-sli-suitability fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::


#### **Recent period: 2001--2025**

During 2001–2025, cold-stress trends became more mixed, with increases across portions of the northern and central interior and decreases in scattered areas (@fig-sli-cold-stress-recent). Heat-stress changes remained localized to the Southwest (@fig-sli-heat-stress-recent). Climatic-suitability changes were essentially absent at the P < 0.1 level (@fig-sli-suitability-recent).


